    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d161">

                <pb facs="0253" n="133" sameAs="frus:frus1905/pg133"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chinese Minister to the
                            Acting Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Imperial Chinese Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1905-08-15"><hi rendition="#italic">August 15,
                        1905</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 66.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to inform you that I have
                    received a telegram from the Waiwu Pu, the foreign office at Peking, of which
                    the following is a translation: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p rendition="#right"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Peking</hi>, <hi
                                rendition="#italic">August 14, 1905.</hi></p>
                        <p rendition="#flush">Chinese Minister <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Liang</hi>, <hi rendition="#italic">Washington:</hi></p>
                        <p>The grand council of state, which has just transmitted to this office
                            copies of three imperial decrees issued this year and last with
                            reference to the Hankau-Canton Railway authorizing Chang Chih-t’ung to
                            take all necessary measures for the regaining of the control of the
                            railway, <pb facs="0254" n="134" xml:id="pg134"/> states that, China
                            having given Viceroy Chang authority to do what is necessary in this
                            matter, and imperial sanction having been given to the request of
                            Viceroy Chang presented in a special memorial that Chentung Liang-Cheng
                            act jointly with him, Minister Liang therefore has authority to do what
                            is necessary in this matter.</p>
                        <p>You will please act in accordance with this telegraphic instruction.</p>
                        <p rendition="#right"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Waiwu Pu.</hi></p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>I shall be greatly obliged if you will be good enough to lay the above-given
                    telegram before the President for his information.</p>
                <p>Accept, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Chentung
                    Liang-Cheng.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
